  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — prudent means showing good judgement about the future. Reckless is its opposite, and wealth and silence are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — by the end of the year marks completion by a future point, so the future perfect will have completed is required.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — transient means lasting only a short time, which is temporary. Permanent is its opposite, and distant and noisy are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — the third conditional takes had plus the participle in the if-clause. Left belongs to the second conditional, leaves to the first, and would leave never appears in the if-clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — ambiguous means open to more than one interpretation. Clear is its opposite, and lengthy and correct are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* A — obstinate means refusing to change one's mind. Obedient is its opposite, cheerful is unrelated, and timid describes fear rather than insistence.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — rigid means unbending, so its opposite is flexible. Stiff is a synonym, strong describes strength, and straight describes shape.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — the idiom means to reveal a secret. Losing an animal is the literal reading, and the other two belong to different idioms.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — candid means open and honest in speech. Secretive and shy are its opposites, and rude is a possible effect rather than the meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — reluctant means unwilling. Eager and ready are its opposites, and angry names an emotion the word does not carry.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — it's means it is, and the semicolon joins the two clauses. A leaves a comma splice, C uses a form that does not exist, and D has no mark between the clauses.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — neither is singular, so was. Were, are and have been are plural forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — ambiguous means open to more than one interpretation. Clear is its opposite, and lengthy and correct are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* A — a distance used as one quantity takes a singular verb: was. Were, have and are would count the kilometres separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* C — together with does not make the subject plural, so the verb follows manager and is singular: is. Are, were and have been would need the manager and his assistants joined by and.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — work is uncountable and singular, so the verb is was. Were, have been and are are plural forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — abundant means plentiful, so its opposite is scarce. Plentiful is a synonym, and cheap and hidden describe other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — scarce means in short supply, so its opposite is abundant. Rare is close in meaning, and cheap and hidden describe other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — after to the bare infinitive is required: to submit. Submission is a noun, submitted is past, and submitting is the present participle.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — the roofing was complete before the rains came, so the past perfect passive had been roofed is required. Has been is present perfect, is is present, and was roofing makes the house the roofer.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* A — the first conditional takes the present in the if-clause and will in the main clause, so rains. Rained belongs to the second conditional, had rained to the third, and would rain never appears in an if-clause of this type.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — the number of means the count, which is singular, so has. Have, were and are would agree with applicants rather than with number.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — all the students is plural, so have seen. Has seen, was seeing and is seeing are singular forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* A — the committee is the doer, so the active future will submit is required. Will be submitted makes the committee the thing submitted, submit needs a plural subject, and are submitting is plural and present.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — friendly describes a noun, as in a friendly teacher. The other three are adverbs of manner.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* C — hardly means almost not and modifies a verb. Lovely, lonely and friendly end in -ly but describe nouns, so they are adjectives.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — affect is the verb, so will affect is correct. Effect is normally a noun, affects would need a third-person singular subject in the present, and effected means brought about.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* C — will becomes would after a past reporting verb, and the following day is in the future of that past moment, so would submit. Submits and has submitted keep a present tense, and had submitted puts the action before the statement.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — meticulous means giving great attention to detail. Careless and hasty are its opposites, and generous describes another quality.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* B — each other is used of two, one another of more than two. A therefore misuses the pronoun, and the other two are not English constructions.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — equipment is a mass noun and singular, so is. The plural forms give a mass noun a number it does not have.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — frequent means happening often, so its opposite is rare. Regular, often and usual are synonyms or near-synonyms.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — quickly answers how he ran, so it modifies the verb. The boy and the shop are nouns, and little already modifies boy.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — diligent means showing steady effort. Lazy is its opposite, and intelligent and wealthy name other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* B — frugal means careful with money. Wasteful and generous are its opposites, and wealthy describes having money rather than managing it.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* C — two independent clauses plus one dependent clause. A is simple, B is compound, and D contains a participial phrase.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* B — a sum of money is one amount, so the singular is is required. Are, were and have been would count the naira individually.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* B — praise means to express approval, so its opposite is criticise. Commend and admire are synonyms.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* B — crisis is Greek and changes -is to -es: crises. Crisises is a regularised error, crisi does not exist, and crisis is singular.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 24.26pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* B — the idiom means to ignore or treat someone coldly. Catching a chill is the literal reading, and the other two belong to different expressions.
